{"text":"{
  "status": "NEEDS_IMPROVEMENT",
  "core_findings": [
    "Electronics has the weakest sampled training separation: 9.01% of negatives outrank positives and 22.31% of margins fall below 0.5. Industrial_and_Scientific is next weakest, while CDs_and_Vinyl shows strong separation.",
    "Electronics has the greatest mean embedding alignment (0.7006), followed by Industrial_and_Scientific (0.6592); these indicate directional alignment, not proof of representation collapse."
  ],
  "key_implications": [
    "The model separates sampled positives and negatives unevenly across datasets, with the most ambiguous cases in Electronics.",
    "Strong CDs_and_Vinyl training margins do not establish validation HR@10 or full-catalog ranking quality."
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "headline_metrics": {
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": 0.0427,
      "Electronics:pos_neg_small_margin_rate": 0.2231,
      "Electronics:embedding_collapse_score": 0.7006,
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": 0.0617,
      "CDs_and_Vinyl:pos_neg_margin_p10": 1.7015
    },
    "brief_metric_read": {
      "Electronics:pos_neg_margin_p10": "Lower-tail separation is near zero.",
      "Electronics:pos_neg_small_margin_rate": "22.31% of sampled margins are below 0.5.",
      "Electronics:embedding_collapse_score": "Highest mean directional alignment among the four datasets.",
      "Industrial_and_Scientific:pos_neg_neg_beats_pos_rate": "Sampled negatives outrank positives in 6.17% of valid positions.",
      "CDs_and_Vinyl:pos_neg_margin_p10": "Even the lower-tail sampled margin is strongly positive."
    }
  }
}"}
